| Difficulty | Product | Policy audit | KB audit |
|---|---|---|---|
| `site_lease_rent_and_renewals` | lease | PASS after 4 rounds | PASS |
| `utility_pass_through_billing` | utility | PASS in round 1 | PASS |
| `home_loan_servicing` | loan | PASS after 3 rounds | PASS after a re-render |
| `in_place_home_sale_approvals` | resale | PASS after 3 rounds | PASS after a re-render |
| `community_rules_and_site_care_charges` | rules | PASS after 2 rounds | PASS |

`storage` and `insurance` are fully specified products that no difficulty uses. As the pipeline intends, they were checked only by the render gates and never audited.

**Things worth knowing:**
- **Most reworks were about pricing factors.** Auditors mainly flagged factors labelled "method" that only swapped a number into the same formula, tiers that could never apply, and seeded records that let an agent copy the answer. Every round was closed and re-audited by the same auditor that raised it.
- **I sent one passing item back to its source.** The resale KB audit passed, but it found a rule and its calculation in d4's policy disagreeing on whether a payoff statement counts for a paid-off loan. No scored value depended on it. I treated it as a source error anyway: d4 was fixed, re-audited, re-rendered and re-audited, and all scored values stayed the same.
- **One known issue was left in the frozen blueprint**, as the process requires. A d1 observation (O1) points at base text; both the reworker and the auditor confirmed it moves no scored value, so it is logged in `worklog.md` as `BLUEPRINT-KNOWN`.
- **Realism caveat.** Several instantiate agents said that many fee and percentage tiers are operator policy they wrote themselves, not standard industry figures. The audits judged these consistent and uniquely solvable, but they did not check them against real-world practice.
- **Account cuts did not cost any finished work.** Several agents were cut off by account limits. Each time I checked what was on disk and resumed the same agent, so nothing finished was redone.

I kept `.audit_views/` in the scenario folder: the masked task views, gate snapshots and a `db_query.py` script that let the KB auditors query each task's database through the runtime tools. The audit reports cite these files by hash; erlbench does not load this folder. `worklog.md` has the full timeline and ends with a `DELIVERED` line. I also saved a short memory note on the database query script and on keeping scratch scripts in their own `/tmp` folder.
